[t=003 webapp:email_client] send_message sequence=3 source=agent params={'to': ['hannah.reyes@oakridge-msd.org'], 'cc': [], 'bcc': [], 'subject': '', 'body': "Aiden Mercer finished Holes in two days after thinking reading was boring, and he links Stanley's desert notebook to the journal he keeps at night. Wanting to write his own story this summer is a clear and welcome next step.\n\nAva Patel's essay says this is the first time she has said the word out loud: her dad came back from rehab last spring, and she thinks her mom is finally sleeping again. That directness is brave, specific writing.\n\nBrianna Okafor writes about Sundays with her grandma in the kitchen, Igbo and English meeting over a recipe they both understand. The jollof never being the same twice, and that being the whole point, gives the piece a real idea.\n\nCarlos Reyes-Vega writes that when his brother got deported his mom stopped sleeping, and that he walks his little sister to school. Feeling thirty at twelve, with books as the only place he gets to be a kid again, is the heart of the piece.\n\nDaniela Cruz learned tamales from scratch with her abuela and calls patience its own ingredient. She carries that into the essay: you cannot rush the corn husk, and she lets that comparison do the work.\n\nEthan Williams's best line is that experiments don't care if his brain is fast or slow. He also describes school as easier now and quieter in his head, and that science preference comes through as his own voice.\n\nFatima Hassan chose Inside Out and Back Again because Thanhha Lai's poems feel short and sideways, the way she actually thinks. Wanting to try poems in Somali before she has every word for a feeling is an ambitious and specific goal.\n\nGrace Lindgren's journal started as dates and sad faces and now holds paragraphs and even a poem. She is proud of that, and the growth is visible on the page.\n\nHiro Tanaka and his dad read The Hobbit out loud on Sunday mornings, one chapter at a time, with Hiro as Bilbo and his dad doing the dwarf voices. They keep laughing and starting over, and that joy is the best part of the writing.\n\nImani Brooks's brain moves faster than her pencil, and voice-to-text on the iPad changed her essays so they sound like her when she talks. That solution is explained clearly, and it is the most convincing part of the piece.\n\nJamal Foster takes Mr. Davis's line that anger is energy with nowhere to go and shows he has started running before school. He is sorry, and he says so in a way that sounds sincere.\n\nKalea Wong writes about the four weeks after her mom's mastectomy, when she helped with the drains, and says she learned more about being a person then than in any class. She would write a whole book about her mom, who is okay now, and that purpose lifts the essay.\n\nLiam O'Sullivan explains that mornings are hard because getting his little brother, who has autism, out the door takes everything they have. He is not making excuses, and the writing keeps that distinction clear.\n\nMaya Goldberg calls speech class on Tuesdays her favorite, even if some kids think it is babyish. Ms. Cohen is teaching her to slow down so the words come out the way she means them, and she can see herself getting better.\n\nNoah Bennett is honest about a hard quarter and choices he is not proud of. He is talking with his mom more, including on Wednesdays, and he says writing this down is part of trying to do better.\n\nOlivia Nguyen read 47 books this quarter, and Wrinkle in Time matters to her because Meg has trouble with people too and it works out. Her own story, about a girl who can hear what plants are thinking, is aimed at 200 pages and shows real ambition.\n\nPriya Sharma and her little sister built a reading fort with blankets and flashlights, and they read Charlotte's Web there. She cried at the end even though she is almost a teenager, and she calls caring about characters a kind of bravery.\n\nRyan Kim writes that basketball practice is the only part of the day his brain goes quiet. He is trying to prove his coach right that discipline on the court shows up in the classroom, and that link is concrete.\n\nSofia Martinez moved in August, and the new house, new friends, and new lunch table still feel temporary. She is proud she finished the essay in English without asking her mom for help, because she can erase what she cannot yet say.\n\nTyler Johnson describes mornings that are loud and rushed because his stepdad works nights, and he is not trying to be late. He likes this class because he can write about real stuff instead of made-up prompts, and that preference is the liveliest part of the piece.\n\nUma Desai learned from cooking with her dad that recipes are instructions you can argue with, and she uses that to frame a poem she rewrote three times. The third version sounded like her, not like a recipe, which is real revision.\n\nVictor Chen catches every third word when people talk fast in the hallways, so the essay matters: in writing he can take his time and say exactly what he means. Four drafts, and his decision that this is okay, show patience with the work.\n\nWilla Anderson writes about the night in the barn when Scout had colic and they almost lost her, and she describes fear as cold hands. Scout is fine now, and Willa still checks on her before school, which keeps the ending grounded.\n\nXavier Thompson tried writing a script instead of his short videos and found that dialogue is weird on paper, harder than he expected. He knows the rules and kept this out of class, and that honesty makes the piece useful.\n\nYara Ali writes that Ramadan fell during state testing week and that she was tired every morning, treating fasting as patience practice. Her friends asked questions and she liked explaining instead of hiding, which makes the essay generous.\n\nZach Morrison used to think the quiet room and extra time meant he was dumb, and this piece shows him letting that idea go. He is starting to believe Ms. Reyes that slow and careful beats fast and sloppy.\n\nAmira Khalil pretends to be asleep while her parents argue about money in Arabic, and that scene gives the essay its stake. She wrote it in English, the language of school, while knowing some feelings only exist in Arabic, and she may try both next time.\n\nBrandon Lee writes that he acts out when he is confused because asking for help feels like admitting defeat. The essay's center is the first time he raised his hand, the answer was wrong, and nobody laughed, which mattered more than being right.\n\nChloe Davis writes about Rusty, a pit bull who waited six months at the shelter for adoption, and about Saturdays when the dogs do not care about grades. She cried in front of the whole class and decided that was fine, which is a brave close.\n\nDante Romano calls lunch the hardest period because the cafeteria is loud and nobody saves seats. He eats in the library on Tuesdays and Thursdays and writes about building a routine that works, instead of pretending lunch is easy.\n\nElena Vasquez's essay is for her grandma, who watches her after school while her mom works doubles at the diner and tells stories about crossing the border that Elena is only now old enough to understand. Her grandma cannot read English, and that purpose holds the piece together.\n\nFinn Murphy writes that they lost their apartment in October and stayed with his aunt for two months, and that he did not tell anyone at school until Ms. Reyes asked how he was doing and actually waited. One adult who knows helps, and he lets that last line carry the piece.\n\nGia Washington's chest gets tight during presentations, so she asked to record hers instead of standing up, and Ms. Reyes said yes. The video was shaky, the words were hers, and counting that as growth is a fair reading of the work.\n\nHolden Price thought the assignment was dumb until he started writing about his grandfather's workshop, where he builds furniture without nails. He links that patience to essays and to woodworking, and the comparison is worth keeping.\n\nIsla Fernandez learned ukulele on YouTube over winter break and found that three chords cover most pop songs. She plays for her little brother when he is scared at night because music is easier to share than feelings, and that reason gives the piece its warmth.\n\nJasper Cole knows spell-check saves his life but cannot fix ideas. He dictated the essay to the iPad and then edited it himself, and he cares more whether it sounds honest than whether the mistakes are gone.", 'reply_to': None} status=200 email_id=message-bd1358a4f2 recipients=['hannah.reyes@oakridge-msd.org'] attachments=[]
