an her pencil, and voice-to-text on the iPad changed her essays so they sound like her when she talks. That solution is explained clearly, and it is the most convincing part of the piece. Jamal Foster takes Mr. Davis's line that anger is energy with nowhere to go and shows he has started running before school. He is sorry, and he says so in a way that sounds sincere. Kalea Wong writes about the four weeks after her mom's mastectomy, when she helped with the drains, and says she learned more about being a person then than in any class. She would write a whole book about her mom, who is okay now, and that purpose lifts the essay. Liam O'Sullivan explains that mornings are hard because getting his little brother, who has autism, out the door takes everything they have. He is not making excuses, and the writing keeps that distinction clear. Maya Goldberg calls speech class on Tuesdays her favorite, even if some kids think it is babyish. Ms. Cohen is teaching her to slow down so the words come out the way she means them, and she can see herself getting better. Noah Bennett is honest about a hard quarter and choices he is not proud of. He is talking with his mom more, including on Wednesdays, and he says writing this down is part of trying to do better. Olivia Nguyen read 47 books this quarter, and Wrinkle in Time matters to her because Meg has trouble with people too and it works out. Her own story, about a girl who can hear what plants are thinking, is aimed at 200 pages and shows real ambition. Priya Sharma and her little sister built a reading fort with blankets and flashlights, and they read Charlotte's Web there. She cried at the end even though she is almost a teenager, and she calls caring about characters a kind of bravery. Ryan Kim writes that basketball practice is the only part of the day his brain goes quiet. He is trying to prove his coach right that discipline on the court shows up in the classroom, and that link is concrete. Sofia Martinez moved in August,