ge of school, while knowing some feelings only exist in Arabic, and she may try both next time. Brandon Lee writes that he acts out when he is confused because asking for help feels like admitting defeat. The essay's center is the first time he raised his hand, the answer was wrong, and nobody laughed, which mattered more than being right. Chloe Davis writes about Rusty, a pit bull who waited six months at the shelter for adoption, and about Saturdays when the dogs do not care about grades. She cried in front of the whole class and decided that was fine, which is a brave close. Dante Romano calls lunch the hardest period because the cafeteria is loud and nobody saves seats. He eats in the library on Tuesdays and Thursdays and writes about building a routine that works, instead of pretending lunch is easy. Elena Vasquez's essay is for her grandma, who watches her after school while her mom works doubles at the diner and tells stories about crossing the border that Elena is only now old enough to understand. Her grandma cannot read English, and that purpose holds the piece together. Finn Murphy writes that they lost their apartment in October and stayed with his aunt for two months, and that he did not tell anyone at school until Ms. Reyes asked how he was doing and actually waited. One adult who knows helps, and he lets that last line carry the piece. Gia Washington's chest gets tight during presentations, so she asked to record hers instead of standing up, and Ms. Reyes said yes. The video was shaky, the words were hers, and counting that as growth is a fair reading of the work. Holden Price thought the assignment was dumb until he started writing about his grandfather's workshop, where he builds furniture without nails. He links that patience to essays and to woodworking, and the comparison is worth keeping. Isla Fernandez learned ukulele on YouTube over winter break and found that three chords cover most pop songs. She plays for her little brother when he is scare